**New `lib/api/client.dart` design:**

```dart
class DshApi {
  // unary: POST /api/<ns>/<method>, payload {args}
  Future<dynamic> rpc(String endpoint, [Map<String,dynamic> args = const {}]);
}

class DshConnection {
  // one mux WS socket (/api/remote.mux)
  // openStream(endpoint, args) -> Stream<dynamic> of items (handles open/cancel/end/error frames, streamId routing)
  // readiness: mux socket open + opening $events stream ready frame (host facts) — or a cheap unary call?
}
```

The reconnect/generation logic stays similar. Key new piece: a MuxSocket class managing logical streams over one physical WS:
- send {type:'open', streamId, endpoint, payload:{args}}
- route incoming frames by streamId to per-stream controllers
- on socket close: fail all streams → connection generation rebuild

Then providers consume:
- roster: workspace/follow stream (baseline + increments) — replaces workspace.list + host frames roster events... but session.list is separate (session/list unary + ? stream for session roster changes). Old HostFrame had session/created etc. New: maybe $events emit frames carry those (api-session/added etc. from dsh-api-remotes!). The "api-session/added", "api-session/removed", "api-session/status", "api-session/activity" events — these are the $events emit notifications! So roster refresh = listen to $events emits for api-session/* + workspace/follow increments.
- chat: session/follow stream per session (snapshot + live events), session/control for queue/jobs/projection.
- approvals/questions: $events waterfall frames + $events/result answers? OR still session-level approval/asked events + some respond endpoint. Old app used approval/requested frames + POST /api/respond. New: the $events waterfall with event names like "approval/request", "user-questions/request" (from dsh-api-remotes strings). Answers via $events/result.

